  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure centered to the northwest continues to maintain a\ndry low-level airmass across the central Appalachians. Observed\n12Z soundings from Wilmington OH and Pittsburgh PA demonstrate\nvery dry mid and upper levels with precipitable water values\nunder 0.35 inches. \n\nSurface dewpoints this afternoon were reduced below standard \nNBM central guidance, trending closer to HRRR and MOS solutions \nin the mid 40s to lower 50s. Calmer winds and clear skies over \nthe lowlands will support strong nocturnal inversions, allowing \ntypical cold spots away from major rivers to drop into the lower\n40s overnight. Conversely, higher terrain and eastern mountain \nslopes will experience increasing mid-level cloud cover and \ntighter surface pressure gradients associated with a \nretrograding coastal low off the Mid-Atlantic coast. This added \ncloud cover and light wind stirring will limit radiational \ncooling across the Northeast Mountains, keeping ridgetop and \nmountain cold spot low temperatures warmer than previous \nnights, although couldn't completely rule out some very patchy \nfrost in the best protected mountain valleys with any thin spots\nin the clouds.\n\nClear skies and light surface winds this evening will once \nagain foster favorable conditions for radiational cooling and \nriver valley fog formation for most of the lowlands overnight. \nSlight tightening of the pressure gradient and increasing clouds on\nthe western periphery of the offshore storm system may render \nfog coverage somewhat less aggressive than on previous nights \ncloser to the higher terrain. Any valley fog lifts and \ndissipates through 10 AM Sunday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nA large, vertically stacked coastal low moving inland near the \nMid-Atlantic coast will maintain northwesterly flow aloft across\nthe area through Monday night. While the bulk of moisture and \ndynamic lift remain well east of the region, moisture brushing \nthe higher terrain may squeeze out isolated light rain showers \nor drizzle across the highest ridges of the Northeast Mountains \nlate Sunday into Sunday night, with measurable precipitation \nremaining generally under 0.10 inches. Otherwise, quiet and dry \nweather prevails through early next week. \n\nBy Tuesday night into Wednesday, upper-level ridging builds over\nthe eastern states as southwesterly flow resumes across the Ohio\nValley and Metro Valley. This wind shift will initiate a\ndistinct warming trend, pushing afternoon high temperatures into\nthe upper 70s and lower 80s for Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nOverall pretty low confidence beyond Thursday with a substantial\nspread of solutions regarding development of a southern stream\nlow Wednesday/Thursday in the lee of the southern Rockies and a\npotentially more potent northern stream system early next week.",
